.TH NM 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
nm \- print name list (symbol table) of object files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nm
.RB [ \-gpuv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nm
prints the symbol table of each
.I file
operand, one symbol per line, in the form
\(lq\fIvalue type name\fR\(rq.
.I value
is 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits, zero-filled, or 16 spaces for an
undefined symbol.
.I type
is a single letter: uppercase for a global or weak symbol, lowercase
for a local one.
.RS
.nf
A          absolute
B, b       uninitialized data (bss)
C          common
D, d       initialized data
N          a non\-loaded (e.g. debug) section
R, r       read\-only data
T, t       text (code)
U          undefined (always uppercase)
V, W, w    weak
I, i       GNU indirect function
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only ELF64, little-endian object files are read; a file with any other
format is refused with a diagnostic. Neither PE/COFF object files nor
archives are supported: an archive operand (recognized by its
.RB \(lq !<arch>\en \(rq
magic) is refused with a diagnostic rather than treated as an object
file or walked for its members.
.PP
A symbol table entry with no name, a
.B STT_FILE
entry (a compilation-unit filename record), and an AArch64/ARM
assembler-generated mapping symbol (named
.RB \(lq $x \(rq ,
.RB \(lq $d \(rq ,
.RB \(lq $a \(rq ,
or
.RB \(lq $t \(rq ,
optionally followed by
.RB \(lq . \(rq
and a disambiguator) are never printed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-g
Print only external (global or weak) symbols; local symbols are
omitted.
.TP
.B \-p
Do not sort the output; print symbols in the object file's own
symbol-table order.
.TP
.B \-u
Print only undefined symbols.
.TP
.B \-v
Sort output by symbol value (address) instead of by name. Every
undefined (or weak-undefined) symbol sorts before every symbol with a
real value, regardless of address; among symbols with a real value,
ties are broken by name. Has no effect together with
.BR \-p ,
which disables sorting entirely.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an object file. With no operands, a file named
.B a.out
is read. With more than one operand, each file's symbols are preceded
by a
.RI \(lq file :\(rq
header line.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The default sort by name is a plain byte-wise comparison of the raw
symbol name, not locale-collated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was read and its symbols printed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operand could not be opened, was not a recognized ELF64
little-endian object file, was an archive, or had no symbol table.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid option was given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ar (1),
.BR strip (1),
.BR file (1)
